course: dictionary
lesson: 1627
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it. The word chewing is a good example: it can mean crushing food with your teeth, or biting on something again and again.
explain: «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.» Here chewing is about food. Your teeth break food into small pieces so it is soft enough to swallow, and saliva, the spit in your mouth, starts to digest it.
explain: «Puppies chew things when their teeth grow.» This is the second meaning: biting on something again and again. Ask which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the word to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Chewing means crushing food with your teeth to make it soft enough to swallow.
fact: Chewing also means biting on something again and again, like a dog chewing a bone.
fact: Chewing is the first step in digestion: teeth break food into small pieces, and saliva starts to digest it.
fact: «Chewing gum» is a rubbery gum base kneaded with sugar or sweetener and flavour.
fact: «Chew over» means think about something carefully. «Bite off more than you can chew» means take on more than you can manage.
exercise: «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.» What does chewing mean here? | Talking with food in your mouth | *Crushing food with your teeth so it is soft enough to swallow | Spitting food out of your mouth | Biting on a pencil while you think
exercise: A puppy is chewing a sock. What does chewing mean? | Sleeping on the sofa | Eating its dinner slowly | Crushing food with its teeth | *Biting on something again and again
exercise: Max says, «I need to chew over my answer.» What does chew over mean? | Bite something hard | *Think about something carefully | Spit something out | Eat very slowly
test: «Chewing gum is a rubbery gum base kneaded with sugar or sweetener.» In this sentence, what is chewing? | Crushing food to swallow it | Biting on a pencil | *Working a rubbery gum with your teeth, again and again | Biting into a sandwich once
test: Why does chewing slowly help us? | It makes us talk more | Food gets cold | *It helps us avoid choking | It makes food taste sweeter
exam: Priya says chewing only means biting on something hard. Is she right? | Chewing never involves teeth at all | Yes, it only ever means biting on something hard | *No, chewing can also mean crushing food so it is soft enough to swallow | Yes, but only for dogs
